<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Silesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1248 CE (approx.) · capital Wrocław</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Piast duchy of Silesia, created by the partition of Poland in 1138, which opened the region to German settlement and split into many small duchies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> in 1138, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was divided among his sons, and Silesia, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, went to the eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile">Władysław II the Exile</a>. Expelled in 1146, he fled to the Empire; his sons returned in 1163 with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and ruled Silesia as a separate duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a> and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedwig-of-silesia">Saint Hedwig</a> invited German settlers, founded towns under German law and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trzebnica">Trzebnica</a>. Henry I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> tried to reunite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Henry II was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> in 1241, a disaster for the duchy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II</a>'s death his sons divided Silesia in 1248, the start of its fragmentation into numerous small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> duchies. In the 14th century these accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a>, and Silesia passed later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and, in 1742, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-silesia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Silesia" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Duchy_of_Silesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1248 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wrocław</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Władysław II the Exile, Henry I the Bearded, Henry II the Pious</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Polish, Middle High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Silesia, Księstwo śląskie</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1193&amp;focus=duchy-of-silesia" aria-label="Duchy of Silesia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5067 406 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Silesia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5387 662L5457 692M5387 662L5387 662M5387 662L5367 659M5387 662L5408 675"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0M5387 662h0M5367 659h0M5408 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 662h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 662h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wrocław. <a href="/atlas?year=1193&amp;focus=duchy-of-silesia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
